<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toronto</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as York, Upper Canada</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toronto is the capital of the Canadian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s largest city, on the north-west shore of Lake Ontario. Founded as York by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-graves-simcoe">John Graves Simcoe</a> in 1793, it became the capital of Upper Canada and was briefly occupied by American troops in 1813. Renamed Toronto in 1834, it was one of the rotating capitals of the Province of Canada and grew into a centre of industry, finance and culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toronto.jpg" alt="Toronto" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dillan Payne · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toronto_Skyline_from_Snake_Island,_September_11_2026_(01).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Toronto stands on a harbour on the north-western shore of Lake Ontario, on a broad sloping plateau interspersed with rivers, deep ravines and urban forest. The city covers 631 square kilometres and has a 46-kilometre waterfront. The Toronto Islands and Port Lands extend into the lake and shelter the harbour south of downtown. The name was recorded in many spellings. The early form Taronto referred to the Narrows between Lake Simcoe and Lake Couchiching, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> called tkaronto, meaning where there are trees standing in the water, a name recorded as early as 1615 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Indigenous peoples have inhabited the area for more than 10,000 years. The Wendat, an Iroquoian-speaking agricultural people, farmed it for centuries before European contact, until the Haudenosaunee invaded and massacred them between 1648 and 1650. By the 1660s the Haudenosaunee had two villages in what is now Toronto, Ganatsekwyagon on the Rouge River and Teiaiagon on the Humber River. By 1701 the Mississaugas, an Anishinaabe-speaking people from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, had displaced them at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a>. A portage route from Lake Ontario to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-huron">Lake Huron</a>, the Toronto Carrying-Place Trail, led to the wide use of the name.</p><p>French traders founded Fort Rouillé in 1750 but abandoned it in 1759 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. The British defeated the French and their Indigenous allies, and in 1763 the area became part of the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. After the broadly disputed Toronto Purchase, in which the Mississaugas surrendered the area to the British Crown, the British established the town of York in 1793 and later made it the capital of Upper Canada.</p><p>During the War of 1812 York was the site of the Battle of York, which caused heavy damage and a two-week occupation by American troops. It was renamed and incorporated as the City of Toronto in 1834. In 1867, at Canadian Confederation, it became the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. The city grew beyond its original limits by annexation and amalgamation, reaching its present area in 1998. The completion of the St. Lawrence Seaway in 1959 gave ships access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> from the Atlantic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Toronto is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and the most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, with 2,794,356 people at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. About half of its residents were born outside Canada, over 200 ethnic origins are represented, and more than 160 languages are spoken. It is governed by a mayor and a unicameral city council of 25 councillors. It is Canada's largest financial centre and the home of the Toronto Stock Exchange, and of institutions such as the National Ballet of Canada, the Canadian Opera Company and the Toronto Symphony Orchestra.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toronto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ottawa was chosen as capital of the Province of Canada by Queen Victoria in 1857, partly as a compromise between the rival cities of <b>Toronto</b>, Kingston, Montreal and Quebec.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The diaspora has made the turban and the gurdwara familiar in cities from London and <b>Toronto</b> to Vancouver and New York.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dictionary of Old English, published from <b>Toronto</b> since 1983 and now mainly online, generally supersedes it where available; in September 2018 it covered A to I.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Panels and animals are also in museums in Istanbul, Copenhagen, Detroit, Gothenburg, Munich, Vienna, <b>Toronto</b>, New York, Chicago and Boston, and in the Louvre.</blockquote><a href="/work/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest known copy of volume 5, dated 1052, is held in the collection of the Aga Khan and housed in the Aga Khan Museum in <b>Toronto</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-canon-of-medicine">The Canon of Medicine · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1906 the Whaley and Royce company of <b>Toronto</b> published the music with the French text and a first English translation by Thomas Bedford Richardson, and in 1908 Collier's Weekly held a competition for new English words, won by Mercy E. Powell McCulloch, whose version never gained wide…</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-canada">O Canada</a></li></ul></section>
